// Generics in DOT.
//
// DOT has no type parameters either, because type members do their job. A
// generic function takes its type as an object with a type member, and a
// generic type has its parameters as type members. The `[...]` syntax is a
// way of writing just that, as each pair below shows.

// 1. A generic function takes an object whose type member is the type.
// These two are the same function, written both ways:

def first[A](x: A, y: A): A = x
def first2(A: { type T }, x: A.T, y: A.T): A.T = x

println(first[Int](1, 2) + first2(new { type T = Int }, 3, 4))

// Type arguments can be left out: the checker works out the object to pass
// from the other arguments, and since only its type matters, passes
// nothing at run time.
println(first("left", "right"))

// 2. Bounds are bounds on the member: `[A <: Shape]` is
// `(A: { type T <: Shape })`.

type Named = { val name: String }
def greet[A <: Named](x: A): String = "hello, " + x.name
println(greet(new { val name = "dot"; val extra = 7 }))

// 3. A generic type has its parameters as members: `type Box[+A] = ...` is
// `type Box = { this => type A; ... }`, and `Box[Int]` is
// `Box & { type A <: Int }`: a refinement, which can be written out too.

type Box[+A] = { def get(): A }
def box[A](x: A): Box[A] = new Box[A] { def get(): A = x }
val b: Box { type A <: Int } = box(20)
println(b.get() + 1)

// 4. Variance is which bound the refinement uses. `+A` gives `<:`, so a
// box of something more specific is a box of something more general;
// `-A` gives `>:`, the other way round; and a parameter without a sign
// gives `=`, so its argument can't change at all, as a mutable cell's can't.

type Sink[-A] = { def put(x: A): Unit }
val printer: Sink[Int] = new Sink[Int] { def put(x: Int): Unit = println(x) }
val named = new { val name = "box" }
val general: Box[Named] = box(named)
val specific: Box[Top] = general
printer.put(general.get().name.length)

// 5. Since type parameters are just members, a value's type argument is a
// type you can name, through the value: `xs.A` is what a list holds.

import std.list._
val xs = cons(1, cons(2, cons(3, nil())))
def lastOf(ys: List[Int]): ys.A = reverse(ys).head()
println(lastOf(xs) * 100)

// 6. Generic functions are values too: `[A] => ...` is a function that
// takes the type object first.

val pairUp = [A] => (x: A) => new { val left = x; val right = x }
println(pairUp[String]("twin").right)

// 7. Generic code in the standard library is written exactly this way.
println(mkString(map(filter(range(1, 20), x => x % 3 == 0), (x: Int) => "" + x), " "))
